-- allium: 3
--
-- Poodl — custom game links and shared results
--
-- Scope
--   The two things that travel between people. Poodl has no server, so
--   everything that leaves one device does so as text the player carries: a
--   link, or a grid of squares.
--
-- Includes
--   - Creating a custom game from a word the creator chooses: it must be one
--     Poodl accepts, and the link that results hides it.
--   - Passing on the answer of the game on the board, in any mode, without
--     that word being shown to the player who shares it and without their
--     game being disturbed.
--   - Opening a custom link, including the path where the link does not
--     decode.
--   - Sharing a finished game as an emoji grid that gives nothing away, in
--     either palette, placed on the player's clipboard.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Playing the custom game once it starts (game.allium). Opening a link
--     hands game.allium an answer and steps back.
--   - The obfuscation scheme itself. This module states what it must
--     guarantee, not how it works.
--   - URL structure beyond the fact that the token rides in it, and anything
--     about how text reaches the clipboard.
--
-- Dependencies
--   words.allium — the dictionary a custom answer must be in.
--   game.allium — the finished game a grid is rendered from, the game on the
--     board whose answer can be passed on, and the AnswerDrawn hand-off that
--     starts a custom game.
--   settings.allium — high contrast as it applies, which chooses the palette.
--     The effective value, not the raw setting: the device can turn high
--     contrast on, and a grid that read the setting would stop matching the
--     board whenever it did.

use "./words.allium" as words
use "./game.allium" as game
use "./settings.allium" as settings

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Turning an answer into something that can sit in a URL without giving the
-- game away to anyone who reads it.
contract AnswerObfuscation {
    encode: (answer: words/Word) -> String
    decode: (token: String) -> words/Word?
    custom_game_url: (token: String) -> String

    @invariant Roundtrip
        -- decode(encode(w)) is w, for every word in the guess dictionary.

    @invariant TheUrlCarriesTheTokenAndNothingElseAboutTheAnswer
        -- custom_game_url produces a link that opening resolves back to this
        -- token. The answer appears nowhere in it except inside the token,
        -- and the link says nothing else about the word — not its length
        -- beyond the fixed word length, not a letter, not a hint.

    @invariant AnswerIsNotReadableFromTheToken
        -- The token does not contain the answer as text, and the answer
        -- cannot be read off it by eye. Someone who is sent a link and looks
        -- at it learns nothing they could not learn by playing.

    @invariant DecodeRejectsWhatItDidNotProduce
        -- decode returns nothing for a token this scheme did not produce, and
        -- it never invents a word: whatever decodes at all decodes to
        -- words/config.word_length letters. An altered token never decodes
        -- back to the word it was made from, so an alteration is never hidden
        -- by the game coming out the same.

    @invariant AlterationIsDetectedToABound
        -- Past those three, refusing an altered token is an integrity check
        -- and not a proof, because a fixed-length token cannot make it one:
        -- the tokens that decode are a fixed fraction of the strings the
        -- alphabet can spell, so some alteration always lands on another one.
        -- What is promised is the bound. An altered token survives the check
        -- with probability below one in sixty-five thousand, and one that then
        -- names a word the guess dictionary holds — the only kind that starts
        -- a game rather than being refused as an invalid link — below one in a
        -- hundred million. Every other alteration reaches
        -- RejectInvalidCustomLink, which already requires the decoded word to
        -- be in the guess dictionary.

    @guidance
        -- This is obfuscation, not security. It has to survive idle curiosity
        -- and a glance at the address bar, not a determined attacker: the
        -- answer is in the link by necessity, because there is no server to
        -- keep it on. A player who is determined to cheat can always read the
        -- code that decodes it.
}

-- Rendering a finished game as the grid people paste into messages.
contract ShareGridFormat {
    render_share_grid: (finished_game: game/Game, palette: SharePalette) -> String

    @invariant Heading
        -- The first line is config.share_heading; then, when the game's mode
        -- is custom and not otherwise, a space and config.custom_marker; then
        -- a space, the number of attempts used, a slash and
        -- game/config.max_attempts — "Poodl 4/6", or "Poodl custom 4/6". A
        -- game that was lost uses "X" in place of the number: "Poodl X/6".

    @invariant NoLinkTravelsWithTheGrid
        -- The rendered text carries no URL. Nothing in a shared result lets a
        -- reader open the game, and nothing in a custom result passes that
        -- word on to whoever the message eventually reaches.

    @invariant OneRowPerSubmittedGuess
        -- After the heading comes one row per submitted guess, in ascending
        -- position order, each row holding one tile per letter position in
        -- left-to-right order. Attempts the player never used produce no row.

    @invariant TilesFollowTheMarks
        -- Each tile is chosen by that position's mark. In the standard
        -- palette: correct is config.standard_correct_tile, present is
        -- config.standard_present_tile. In the high-contrast palette: correct
        -- is config.high_contrast_correct_tile, present is
        -- config.high_contrast_present_tile. Absent is config.absent_tile in
        -- both.

    @invariant NeverRevealsTheAnswer
        -- No letter of the answer, and no letter of any guess, appears
        -- anywhere in the rendered text. The grid says how the game went, not
        -- what the word was.

    @invariant Deterministic
        -- The same finished game and palette always render the same text.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum SharePalette { standard | high_contrast }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    share_heading: String = "Poodl"

    -- What marks a grid as coming from someone else's word. It names the
    -- mode and says nothing about the answer.
    custom_marker: String = "custom"

    standard_correct_tile: String = "🟩"
    standard_present_tile: String = "🟨"
    high_contrast_correct_tile: String = "🟧"
    high_contrast_present_tile: String = "🟦"
    absent_tile: String = "⬛"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Making a custom game ---------------------------------------------------

-- The creator's word has to be one Poodl would accept as a guess, or the
-- recipient could never type it.
--
-- Nothing is recorded. The link is handed to the creator and that is the end
-- of Poodl's involvement: there is no entity, no history and nothing that
-- survives a reload.
rule CreateCustomGameLink {
    when: PlayerCreatesCustomGame(player, entry)

    let text = lowercase(entry)
    let candidate = words/Word{text: text}

    requires: exists candidate
    requires: candidate.in_guess_dictionary

    let token = encode(candidate)

    ensures: CustomLinkReady(player: player, url: custom_game_url(token))
}

rule RejectCustomAnswer {
    when: PlayerCreatesCustomGame(player, entry)

    let text = lowercase(entry)
    let candidate = words/Word{text: text}

    requires: not exists candidate or not candidate.in_guess_dictionary

    ensures: CustomAnswerRejected(player: player, entry: entry)
}

-- Passing on the answer in play ------------------------------------------

-- The other way a custom link comes about: not a word the player chose, but
-- the one their own game is built on. It works the same from the share dialog
-- the header opens, where it sits beside CreateCustomGameLink, and from the
-- end-of-game modal, and in every mode — including a custom game, so a word
-- that was sent to somebody can be sent on by them.
--
-- Nothing is recorded here either. As with CreateCustomGameLink, the link is
-- handed over and that is the end of it.
rule ShareCurrentAnswerAsCustomGame {
    when: PlayerSharesCurrentAnswer(player, current_game)

    requires: current_game.is_current

    let token = encode(current_game.answer)

    ensures: CustomLinkReady(player: player, url: custom_game_url(token))

    @guidance
        -- The game is a parameter, not a target. Nothing in ensures touches
        -- it: sharing spends no attempt, retires nothing and changes no
        -- status, so the board is exactly as it was afterwards.
        --
        -- There is no rejection counterpart to this rule, and no validation
        -- to do. Unlike CreateCustomGameLink, which is handed a word by a
        -- person, this answer came from Poodl, and game.allium's
        -- AnswersAreAlwaysGuessable already holds it in the guess dictionary.
        --
        -- The binding is called current_game rather than game because this
        -- module imports game.allium under that alias.
}

-- Opening a custom link --------------------------------------------------

-- The link decodes, so that word becomes the answer. game.allium takes it
-- from here: it retires whatever game was on the board, which is why opening
-- a link can cost the player a streak, and starts the custom game.
rule OpenCustomGameLink {
    when: PlayerOpensCustomLink(player, token)

    let decoded = decode(token)

    requires: exists decoded
    requires: decoded.in_guess_dictionary

    ensures: game/AnswerDrawn(player: player, mode: custom, answer: decoded)
}

-- The link is malformed, was altered on the way, or names a word this build
-- does not have. The player is told, and offered an ordinary game instead of
-- being left on a dead end.
rule RejectInvalidCustomLink {
    when: PlayerOpensCustomLink(player, token)

    let decoded = decode(token)

    requires: not exists decoded or not decoded.in_guess_dictionary

    ensures: CustomLinkInvalid(player: player)
}

rule PlayerAcceptsRandomFallback {
    when: PlayerAcceptsRandomFallback(player)

    ensures: game/NewGameRequested(player: player, mode: random)
}

-- Sharing a result -------------------------------------------------------

rule PlayerSharesResults {
    when: PlayerSharesResults(player, finished)

    requires: finished.is_finished_by_play

    let palette = if settings/player_settings.high_contrast_active: high_contrast else: standard
    let text = render_share_grid(finished, palette)

    ensures: ResultsPlacedOnClipboard(player: player, finished_game: finished, text: text)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface CustomGameCreation {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    provides:
        PlayerCreatesCustomGame(viewer, entry)

    contracts:
        fulfils AnswerObfuscation
        fulfils game/DirectManipulation

    -- The other half of the dialog this is offered in: passing on the word of
    -- the game on the board sits beside it, for as long as there is one.
    related:
        ShareCurrentAnswer(viewer.current_game)
            when viewer.current_game != null

    @guarantee OnlyAcceptedWordsBecomeCustomGames
        -- A word that is not in the guess dictionary produces no link. The
        -- rejection says so in a way that is perceivable both visually and to
        -- assistive technology, and the entry stays put for the creator to
        -- correct.

    @guarantee TheLinkIsTheWholeGame
        -- The link produced carries everything the recipient needs. There is
        -- no server: nothing about this custom game is recorded anywhere the
        -- recipient could reach, so a link that is lost is gone.

    @guarantee TheWordIsNotReadableInTheLink
        -- The answer travels obfuscated. Neither the link nor anything shown
        -- alongside it displays the word once the link has been made, and the
        -- word is hidden from the player who opens it until their game ends.

    @guarantee NothingAboutTheLinkIsKept
        -- The link exists long enough for the creator to copy it and no
        -- longer. Poodl keeps no record of a custom game it has made: nothing
        -- survives a reload, and there is no list of the words a player has
        -- set for other people. A link that is lost is gone, which is the
        -- same thing TheLinkIsTheWholeGame says from the other direction.

    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Entering a word, submitting it and copying the resulting link can
        -- all be done from the keyboard alone.
}

-- Handing on the word the player is playing. One surface covers the share
-- dialog and the end-of-game modal both, because is_current spans a game in
-- progress and one just finished, and an abandoned game is never on the board
-- to share. During play the way in is the dialog the header opens, shared
-- with CustomGameCreation; once the game is over the modal offers it too.
surface ShareCurrentAnswer {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    context current_game: game/Game where player = viewer and is_current

    exposes:
        current_game.mode
        current_game.status

    provides:
        PlayerSharesCurrentAnswer(viewer, current_game)

    contracts:
        fulfils AnswerObfuscation
        fulfils game/DirectManipulation

    -- No related clause names game.allium's surfaces. The language reference
    -- shows no related clause crossing modules and the checker resolves none,
    -- so the adjacency those entries carried is stated in
    -- AvailableInEveryModeAndForAsLongAsTheGameIsOnTheBoard instead.

    @guarantee TheAnswerIsNeverShownInOrderToShareIt
        -- current_game.answer is deliberately absent from the exposed set.
        -- Making a link displays nothing about the word — not a letter, not
        -- its position, not a hint — so a player who shares part way through
        -- learns nothing beyond what their own guesses have already revealed,
        -- exactly as game.allium's AnswerIsNeverExposedWhileInProgress
        -- requires. Once the game is over the conclusion has already shown the
        -- answer; sharing still adds nothing of its own.

    @guarantee SharingCostsTheGameNothing
        -- No attempt is spent, no game is retired, no status changes, no
        -- streak moves and nothing is counted. A game in progress carries on
        -- untouched with its typed letters still on the board, and a finished
        -- one keeps its conclusion on screen.

    @guarantee TheRecipientDoesNotSeeTheWordEither
        -- The link travels obfuscated on the terms AnswerObfuscation states,
        -- and opening it starts a game whose answer stays hidden until that
        -- game ends, on the terms CustomLinkEntry states. Neither end of the
        -- exchange is told the word.

    @guarantee AvailableInEveryModeAndForAsLongAsTheGameIsOnTheBoard
        -- Random, endless, practice and custom alike, from before the first
        -- guess through to after the game is over. Nothing here depends on
        -- having guessed anything, or on how the game turned out.
        -- game.allium's GameBoard is reachable from here for as long as the
        -- game is current, and its GameConclusion as well once the game is
        -- finished by play.

    @guarantee AWordSomebodySentYouCanBeSentOn
        -- A custom game's answer can be shared onward like any other. The
        -- creator chose who received their link and not who receives it after
        -- that, and Poodl does not try to hold that line: there is no server
        -- that could, and the word is only ever as private as the people
        -- holding it.

    @guarantee NothingAboutTheLinkIsKept
        -- The link exists long enough for the player to copy it and no longer.
        -- Poodl keeps no record of one: no list of the answers a player has
        -- passed on, and nothing that survives a reload.

    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Sharing and copying the resulting link can be done from the keyboard
        -- alone, and whether the copy succeeded is reported both visually and
        -- to assistive technology.
}

surface CustomLinkEntry {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    provides:
        PlayerOpensCustomLink(viewer, token)
        PlayerAcceptsRandomFallback(viewer)

    contracts:
        fulfils AnswerObfuscation
        fulfils game/DirectManipulation

    @guarantee OpeningALinkStartsThatGame
        -- A link that decodes starts a custom game on that word straight
        -- away, replacing whatever was on the board. If the game being
        -- replaced was under way and had a guess in it, that counts as a loss
        -- in exactly the way starting any other game would.

    @guarantee InvalidLinksAreExplainedAndSurvivable
        -- A link that does not decode tells the player it is not a Poodl link
        -- rather than failing silently or starting some other game, and
        -- offers a random game as a way out. The explanation is perceivable
        -- both visually and to assistive technology.
}

surface ShareResults {
    facing viewer: game/Player

    context finished: game/Game
        where player = viewer and is_current and is_finished_by_play

    let palette =
        if settings/player_settings.high_contrast_active: high_contrast
        else: standard

    -- The grid itself, shown and not only copied. TheGridIsAvailableAsText
    -- asks for it to be readable before it is sent, and a clipboard the
    -- browser refuses is a clipboard the player has to work around by hand.
    let text = render_share_grid(finished, palette)

    exposes:
        finished.status
        finished.mode
        finished.attempts_used
        palette
        text

    provides:
        PlayerSharesResults(viewer, finished)

    contracts:
        fulfils game/DirectManipulation
        fulfils ShareGridFormat

    -- No related clause here either, for the reason ShareCurrentAnswer gives:
    -- SharingIsAvailableOnceTheGameIsOver carries the adjacency instead.

    @guarantee SharedTextGivesNothingAway
        -- The text placed on the clipboard names no letter, no word and no
        -- link. A recipient who has not played can read it without having the
        -- game spoiled, which is the whole point of sharing squares rather
        -- than letters. Passing the word itself on is a separate action the
        -- player takes deliberately, on ShareCurrentAnswer; it never rides
        -- along with a grid, so a forwarded message stays a report of how a
        -- game went rather than becoming a way into it.

    @guarantee PaletteFollowsHighContrast
        -- The tiles match what the player sees: the high-contrast palette when
        -- high contrast applies, the standard palette otherwise. It follows
        -- high contrast as it applies rather than as it was set, so a device
        -- asking for more contrast moves the board and the grid together. What
        -- matches is which palette is in use; the emoji are whatever the
        -- reader's platform draws, and never were the board's own colours.

    @guarantee ACustomResultSaysSo
        -- A grid from a custom game is marked as one, so it never reads as
        -- though the player beat a word Poodl chose. The marker names the mode
        -- and nothing else: it says the word came from a link rather than from
        -- Poodl, and reveals nothing about which word it was, nor who set it,
        -- nor whether whoever sent the link knew it themselves.

    @guarantee SharingIsAvailableOnceTheGameIsOver
        -- Sharing is offered for a game the player finished by playing it
        -- out, won or lost, and never while one is still in progress.
        -- game.allium's GameConclusion, the surface that shows a finished
        -- game, is reachable from here. A game that was abandoned is never on
        -- the board to share: it is replaced the instant it is abandoned.

    @guarantee TheGridIsAvailableAsText
        -- What gets copied is text. It is shown as text as well as placed on
        -- the clipboard, so it can be read by assistive technology and
        -- selected by hand before it is sent, and it stays there when the
        -- clipboard cannot be reached. The action reports whether the copy
        -- succeeded.
}
